<item>
  <title>Concepts of virtualisation</title>
  <dc:creator>Jianping Meng</dc:creator>
  <link>https://jpmeng.github.io/virtualcluster/20260502_virtualHPC_concepts.html</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="layers-of-virtualisation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="layers-of-virtualisation">Layers of virtualisation</h2>
<p>There are several layers of virtualisation. Bare Metal is the hardware. A Hypervisor is the software that manages that hardware for VMs. Type 1 (ESXi, KVM, Hyper-V) sits directly on the hardware for maximum speed. Type 2 (VirtualBox) sits on an OS for convenience. QEMU is the “utility player”—it provides the virtual hardware (keyboard/disk) to KVM, or acts as a standalone (but slow) emulator.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Bare Metal: The raw, physical hardware (CPU, RAM, Disk). It’s the “metal” server rack with nothing installed on it yet.</p></li>
<li><p>Hypervisor: The manager. It’s the software layer that sits on the hardware to create and run Virtual Machines (VMs). It’s the “boss” that decides how much RAM each VM gets.</p>
<p>There are two types of hypervisors: Type 1 and Type 2.</p>
<p>Type 1 hypervisors boot directly on the physical server to run multiple virtual machines at maximum efficiency.</p>
<p>They include:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>VMware ESXi: It is its own tiny operating system. When the computer turns on, ESXi loads first, then starts your VMs. There is no “middleman.”</p></li>
<li><p>Microsoft Hyper-V: This is a “stealth” Type 1. When you enable Hyper-V on Windows, it actually slides underneath your Windows OS. Windows itself becomes a “special VM” (management partition), giving Hyper-V direct control of the hardware.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>KVM: It converts the Linux Kernel into the hypervisor. Since the Kernel owns the hardware, KVM operates at the lowest possible level.</p>
<p>Type 2 hypervisors run as an application on top of an existing OS. You use your computer normally, open the hypervisor software, and launch a VM inside a window. Such hypervisors typically include Oracle VirtualBox and VMware Workstation. They are easy to set up and use, while their performance is lower than Type 1 ones in principle.</p>
<p>QEMU is a special one: if working with KVM, it is a part of a Type 1 hypervisor setup, but it can also work alone, which acts as an emulator. When working with KVM, it emulates “extra” hardware the VM needs (like a mouse, keyboard, or disk drive) that isn’t handled by the KVM engine.</p></li>
<li><p>Guest virtual machine: An independent software instance of an operating system that runs on top of a hypervisor.</p></li>
<li><p>Container Engine (Docker/containerd/Apptainer/podman): Runs on the Guest VM (or bare metal) to host containers.</p>
<p>Think of a VM like a standalone house—it has its own plumbing, heating, and infrastructure. A container is like an apartment in a building—it has its own front door and space, but it shares the building’s main infrastructure (the OS).</p>
<p>Virtual Machines: Each VM includes a full copy of an operating system (OS), the application, and necessary drivers. They run on a “hypervisor” that mimics hardware, making them heavy and slow to start, but very secure and isolated.</p>
<p>Containers: Containers share the host’s OS kernel and only package the application and its dependencies. This makes them incredibly lightweight, fast to boot, and highly portable across different environments.</p>
<p>Containers can be created and run inside a VM.</p></li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="management" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="management">Management</h2>
<p>There are also tools for managing virtual machines and containers. Locally, there are libvirt (KVM) and VBoxManage (VirtualBox) for dealing with virtual machines, and Docker CLI, Apptainer CLI, and Podman CLI for dealing with containers. Apart from them, there are also higher-level management tools like OpenStack for managing many virtual machines and K8s for containers.</p>


</section>

